//! # The calibrated `DeviceCMYK` → sRGB node grid (generated data)
//!
//! **GENERATED FILE — do not hand-edit.** Regenerate with:
//!
//! ```text
//! python tools/cmyk-calibration/cmyk_probe.py --levels 9 --engine pdfium //!     --out tools/cmyk-calibration/out/fit-pdfium.tsv
//! python tools/cmyk-calibration/fit.py //!     --fit tools/cmyk-calibration/out/fit-pdfium.tsv --levels 6 --emit-rust
//! ```
//!
//! ## What these numbers are
//!
//! 1,296 sRGB triples: a uniform 6×6×6×6 grid over the `DeviceCMYK` unit
//! hypercube, least-squares fitted to **measured** pdfium render output for a
//! 9-level (6,561-point) CMYK lattice. [`super::cmyk_to_srgb`] quadrilinearly
//! interpolates between them. The full rationale — why a table at all, why
//! this target, why L = 6, and the measured accuracy — is in that module's
//! documentation; this file is only the data.
//!
//! ## Index order (load-bearing)
//!
//! Row-major in **(c, m, y, k)** with `c` slowest and `k` fastest:
//!
//! ```text
//! index = ((ci * 6 + mi) * 6 + yi) * 6 + ki
//! ```
//!
//! The trailing comment on every row states its `(ci, mi, yi, ki)` so a
//! specific ink combination can be grepped directly — `// c5 m0 y0 k0` is
//! solid cyan on paper, `// c0 m0 y0 k5` is solid black ink alone.
//!
//! ## Provenance and licensing (rule 13 / `docs/LEGAL.md` §6)
//!
//! These values were obtained by **rendering and measuring**, not by
//! extracting a table or a curve from an ICC profile. `cmyk_probe.py` emits a
//! synthetic patch-grid PDF, renders it with pdfium via `pypdfium2`, and reads
//! back the centre pixel of each patch; `fit.py` then solves a linear
//! least-squares problem for the node colours. No ICC profile is read,
//! shipped, or redistributed by pdfcer, and no third-party table is copied —
//! which is what keeps this free of the redistribution terms that attach to
//! most real-world CMYK profiles.

